# Release manifest. One file per release; never edit a published one, add a new .<n> instead.
SWIFT_VERSION=6.4.1
RELEASE_ID=6.4.1-hyp3r.5
BASE_IMAGE=swift:6.4.1-noble
BASE_IMAGE_SLIM=swift:6.4.1-noble-slim
LIBDISPATCH_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-corelibs-libdispatch
LIBDISPATCH_REF=295b55fa9c8c85d8a39f8b02dbe800c27ab0f3a1
FOUNDATION_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-corelibs-foundation
FOUNDATION_REF=2c9560ff741797a610e75e88c172d458f79da26d
SWIFT_FOUNDATION_TAG=swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
SWIFT_FOUNDATION_ICU_TAG=swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
SWIFT_COLLECTIONS_TAG=1.1.6
FIXES="Swift 6.4.1 with the hyp3r fixes. SC-0001: libdispatch use-after-free in the Linux epoll backend, SIGSEGV in _dispatch_event_loop_drain (swiftlang/swift-corelibs-libdispatch#949); backport of upstream PR #965 onto swift-6.4.1-RELEASE. SC-0002: Foundation Process on Linux over-reads dirent.d_name in findMaximumOpenFromProcSelfFD, intermittent SIGSEGV (swiftlang/swift-corelibs-foundation#5472); backport of upstream commit 81eb85a2 onto swift-6.4.1-RELEASE. SC-0004: the optimizer moved a class field-offset load above the allocation of the first instance (metadata accessors are modelled as readnone), so the first object of a class with a non-constant layout could get a field store over its isa pointer, intermittent SIGSEGV or silent heap corruption in -O builds; fixed in swift-frontend (LLVM alias analysis of swift_allocObject), the Linux images and tarballs carry the rebuilt compiler SC-0006: #isolation in nonisolated(nonsending) code (the default under NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault) ignored its contextual type, e.g. #isolation === MainActor.shared, so -O builds aborted in the LLVM verifier ('PHINode should have one entry for each predecessor', swiftlang/swift#92798); now converted, and types (any Actor)? cannot convert to are diagnosed like in @MainActor code (#isolation != nil there becomes an error; write (#isolation as (any Actor)?) != nil). SC-0019: #isolation in nonisolated(nonsending) code was consumed without a copy, so e.g. String(describing: #isolation) over-released the calling actor (deinit while running, then SIGSEGV; swiftlang/swift#92799). Both fixed in swift-frontend; the Linux images and tarballs carry the rebuilt compiler. SC-0005: a typed-throws closure passed to a generic untyped-throws parameter (e.g. Task { () async throws(E) -> T in ... }) crashed the compiler for error types with storage and returned a corrupted error for small enums (swiftlang/swift#92312, a 6.4 regression); fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0003: -O builds of async functions that take a small enum or struct whose native explosion is coerced through a stack temporary (e.g. an enum with a tuple payload) could lose their spilled self and arguments: IRGen sized the temporary's lifetime markers in bits instead of bytes, so after the temporary moved into the async frame the optimizer deleted the spills, and the first resume read garbage (SIGSEGV or wrong values); backport of upstream swiftlang/swift#91312 (main only), fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0022: a typed-throws closure whose context type is a type parameter (stored in [() throws(E) -> T], passed through a generic id<T>) crashed the compiler in SILGen; fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0014: on Linux a DispatchSource signal source never fired once the signal had been set to SIG_IGN (or SIG_DFL) again after the first source for it registered (libdispatch installed its sigaction only once per signal, so the kernel discarded the signal); libdispatch now reinstalls its handler on every registration when the disposition is SIG_IGN/SIG_DFL (fix in src/event/event_epoll.c, not upstream yet); also in the static Linux SDK's libdispatch.a. SC-0023: our SC-0006 fix made every call that passes #isolation to an isolated parameter in nonisolated(nonsending) code count as an isolation crossing, so valid code that stock 6.4.1 accepts was rejected ('non-Sendable ... returned from actor-isolated ... to nonisolated context', e.g. for await over non-Sendable elements, Swift Testing's #expect(processExitsWith:), or 'cannot be called from outside of the actor' for synchronous callees); a regression of hyp3r compilers since Linux hyp3r.9 / macOS hyp3r.8, fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0024: with the SC-0001 libdispatch, SwiftPM's plugin runner kept reading a plugin's stdout/stderr pipes at end-of-file in a tight loop (one CPU busy after every plugin run, e.g. +68% CPU per re-plan); usr/bin/swift-package is rebuilt from the patched SwiftPM fork (swift-build, swift-test, swift-run are symlinks to it). sourcekit-lsp links SwiftPM statically and is not rebuilt. SwiftPM: the full Linux images carry the official swift-syntax 604.0.0 prebuilts (swift.org has none for 6.4.1; the 6.4.0-RELEASE files are used, stored under this image's own compiler tag) in /opt/hyp3r/swiftpm-prebuilts, so macro packages that depend on swift-syntax 604.0.0 skip compiling swift-syntax: swift build --experimental-prebuilts-download-url file:///opt/hyp3r/swiftpm-prebuilts (also in HYP3R_SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS_URL). SC-0031: on Linux the libdispatch workqueue monitor timer woke every process once a second for its whole lifetime, also when idle with no worker threads left (battery cost for always-on services); it now runs only while a monitored worker exists, in the shared and the static libdispatch and in the static Linux SDK. Marker string hyp3r-dispatch-workq-monitor-idle-v1 (grep -c) in libdispatch.so, libdispatch.a and statically linked binaries. SC-0032: in actor- or global-actor-isolated code an optional-chained mutation whose later parts await (dict[k]?.x = await f(), ?.x += await f(), ?.append(await f()), also through a non-Sendable class the actor holds) began its access before the await and held it across the suspension, so concurrent tasks on the actor lost their updates and, with class payloads, could hit a use-after-free or SIGSEGV, with no diagnostic; the compiler now warns (diagnostic group ActorIsolatedOptionalChainSuspension, a warning in Swift 5 and 6 modes, promote with -Werror ActorIsolatedOptionalChainSuspension); fix: move the await into a local first. Compiler change over hyp3r.4: SC-0032 only."
# Patched Static Linux SDK (musl): the official bundle below with libdispatch.a and libFoundation.a rebuilt from the refs above.
STATIC_SDK_VERSION=0.1.0
STATIC_SDK_URL=https://download.swift.org/swift-6.4.1-release/static-sdk/swift-6.4.1-RELEASE/swift-6.4.1-RELEASE_static-linux-0.1.0.artifactbundle.tar.gz
STATIC_SDK_SHA256=cef5592d7430ed3c5ae1ddda479849c6f5252bd94a5723db1559f984caf38a7c
# Compiler overlay (SC-0003, SC-0004, SC-0005, SC-0006, SC-0019, SC-0022, SC-0023, SC-0032): swift-frontend rebuilt from the ref below plus the unmodified swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
# LLVM/Clang/cmark/swift-syntax/string-processing trees, release build without assertions, swapped into the official toolchain.
# The static SDK's own libraries do not depend on the compiler fix; code built against it still uses the host compiler.
SWIFT_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift
SWIFT_REF=aa76e006f1b63f0d4b0f800085e9b3f1602ca230
SWIFT_UPSTREAM_TAG=swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
# SwiftPM overlay (SC-0024): usr/bin/swift-package rebuilt from the ref below (swift-6.4.1-RELEASE + the fix), recipe in overlays/swiftpm.
SWIFTPM_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-package-manager
SWIFTPM_REF=4429e8becef360e2a26f4797caa8eb245198f07a
# SwiftPM swift-syntax prebuilts baked into the full images (jobs image, tests/prebuilts-swift-syntax): the official swift.org
# files for SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS_SOURCE_TAG, stored under the compiler tag the built image reports (computed in the build).
# swift.org publishes none for 6.4.1; the 6.4.0 files are used there too (same swiftmodule format, gate-tested).
SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS=swift-syntax:604.0.0
SWIFTPM_PREBUILTS_SOURCE_TAG=swift-6.4.0-RELEASE
